WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
What a working engineer would do.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:04.568
For the project, keep serving boring and measured.

3
00:00:04.848 --> 00:00:06.917
Pick one engine and pin its version.

4
00:00:07.197 --> 00:00:14.088
Serve the base model and your adapter from the same server, so the base-versus-tuned comparison isolates the adapter.

5
00:00:14.368 --> 00:00:19.467
Use schema-constrained decoding for both, plus Pydantic validation on the way back.

6
00:00:19.747 --> 00:00:30.968
Measure throughput and p50 and p95 latency at two or three concurrency levels with a small load script, and record the hardware, precision, and settings next to the numbers.

7
00:00:31.248 --> 00:00:44.859
Then compute cost per 1,000 tasks at the utilization you can honestly expect, and the break-even volume against both the frontier model and the cheapest API model that passes your benchmark.

8
00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:55.613
If the cost case doesn’t hold, say so: a write-up that concludes “fine-tuning matched quality, but a small API model is cheaper at our volume” is a successful benchmark.
